// ===== ROOT-CAUSED 2026-07-18 by v-memory-safety (clean bisect) =====
// NOT the closure-in-generic-variant: it is the `if h == 1` EQUALITY where h is the erased type-param
// value. PROOF: deleting `== 1` -> the generic-variant stored-closure callback RUNS (1 passed). TWO parts:
//  (1) OWNERSHIP [v-memory-safety, small+safe]: value-eq classifies both operands via
//      heap_operand_ownership; the boxed erased/deferred-int constant `1` fell to the `_ => decline`
//      (only a BigInt ConstInt arm existed). Widening `Core::ConstInt => Owned` fixes it (a scalar `=`
//      at concrete width is Core::Compare / i*.eq, never value-eq, so widening is sound).
//  (2) DEEPER [champ_eq/value-eq emit; borders v-inference/erasure]: with (1) applied it compiles past
//      ownership but emits INVALID wasm ("expected i32 found i64" at the value-eq operand) — the ValueEq
//      emit does NOT box a SCALAR operand into a heap handle (emits raw i64.const vs the i32 handle
//      champ_eq expects), so the erased-value compare was LATENTLY WRONG and the decline was masking it.
//      Full fix = ValueEq emit boxes a scalar operand when comparing at heap/erased type + width coercion.
// Part 1 alone un-masks the invalid wasm (worse than the clean decline), so NOT landed piecemeal.
// Sequencing routed to concierge (both-parts-mine vs split part-2 to v-inference).
// =====================================================================

// BACKEND DECLINE ("borrowing op operand has an ownership this backend cannot yet prove") — filed by
// v-iterators 2026-07-18. This is the v-memory-safety-territory ownership decline the concierge flagged as
// the I2 risk; it surfaces even EARLIER than I2 (at the first runtime STEP), and it is the SECOND critical-
// path blocker for the operator's monomorphic/generic/fused adapter-record iterator redesign (after the now-
// FIXED construction bug bca5da9e0). The construction + typecheck now work (that fix landed); this is the
// RUNTIME lowering of calling a closure STORED IN A GENERIC VARIANT.
//
// SYMPTOM: a GENERIC sum variant `Mk(s, s -> Option((a, s)))` — the adapter-record iterator shape, a state
// field `s` plus a stored `step` closure over the type params — CONSTRUCTS + TYPECHECKS fine, but calling
// the stored closure back (`f(s)`) DECLINES at lowering: "borrowing op operand has an ownership this backend
// cannot yet prove". Reproduces at a SINGLE concrete instantiation (not a multi-type / monomorphization-tie
// issue).
//
// DISCRIMINATORS (minimized, verified on trunk ~a26f90b59, cdz built with bca5da9e0):
//   - MONOMORPHIC variant storing the SAME shape closure works + RUNS:
//       `type Box = | Mk(Int64 -> Int64)` called back → PASS.
//       `type St = | Mk(List(Int64), List(Int64) -> Option((Int64, List(Int64))))` called back → PASS.
//     So calling a closure stored in a variant is fine when the variant is MONOMORPHIC.
//   - GENERIC variant `Mk(s, s -> Option((a, s)))` storing a closure over its type params, called back via
//     `f(s)` at ONE concrete instantiation → DECLINES "borrowing op operand ownership". <-- THE BUG.
//   So the trigger is a GENERIC-VARIANT stored-closure callback, NOT monomorphization-at-two-types and NOT
//   the construction bug (that's fixed). Likely the Perceus/borrow-ownership analysis over a closure pulled
//   out of a generic variant field can't prove the operand's ownership when the field type is a type-param-
//   parameterized arrow. v-memory-safety territory (Perceus/FBIP/borrowing-op ownership); v-inference offered
//   to help confirm the locus.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS: `step(s)` on a generic iterator is the FUNDAMENTAL operation of the whole fused-iterator
// redesign — every producer/combinator/consumer calls the stored step. Until a generic-variant stored-closure
// callback lowers, the adapter-record encoding cannot RUN (it type-checks but declines at emit). This gates
// I1 (run the mono spike) and I2 (fusion proof), the operator's re-charter.

// CONTROL A — monomorphic variant, closure callback: RUNS (PASS).
type BoxA = | Mk(Int64 -> Int64)
def callA(b, x) = match b with | BoxA.Mk(f) => f(x)
@test
def mono-closure-in-variant-runs() = if callA(BoxA.Mk(fn(n) => n + 1), 5) == 6 then unit else trap("A")

// THE BUG — generic variant, stored closure over the type params, called back at ONE instantiation:
type Iter = | Mk(s, s -> Option((a, s)))
def step-once(it) = match it with | Iter.Mk(s, f) => f(s)
@test
def generic-variant-closure-callback-runs() =
  match step-once(Iter.Mk([1, 2], fn(s) => match s with
    | [] => Option.None(unit)
    | [h, .. t] => Option.Some((h, t)))) with
  | Option.None(_) => trap("empty")
  | Option.Some(p) => (match p with | (h, _rest) => if h == 1 then unit else trap("head"))
